# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
from nums import *

H1("2. Governing equations")
H2("2.1 Layered medium and MGL thermoelasticity")
P("The slab occupies $0<x<L$ and is divided by the plane $x=h$ into a tumour layer (index 1) and a normal tissue layer (index 2). The displacement $u_i(x,t)$ is along $x$ and the temperature increment above the reference temperature $T_0=T_b=310$ K is $\\theta_i(x,t)$:")
EQ(r"\Omega_1=\{0<x<h\},\qquad \Omega_2=\{h<x<L\},\qquad i=1\ (\text{tumour}),\ i=2\ (\text{normal})", "dom")
P("The coefficient of thermomechanical coupling and the one-dimensional strain are")
EQ(r"\beta_i=(3\lambda_i+2\mu_i)\alpha_{Ti},\qquad e_i=\partial_x u_i", "beta")
P("with $\\lambda_i$ and $\\mu_i$ the Lamé constants and $\\alpha_{Ti}$ the coefficient of linear thermal expansion. In the MGL theory the strain rate is added to the constitutive law with the same lag $\\tau_1$ that multiplies the temperature rate, so that the stress and the equation of motion are")
EQ(r"\sigma_i=\left(1+\tau_1\partial_t\right)\left[(\lambda_i+2\mu_i)\,\partial_x u_i-\beta_i\theta_i\right]", "sig")
EQ(r"\rho_i\,\partial_t^2 u_i=\partial_x\sigma_i", "mot")
P("Eliminating the stress gives the motion equation in the form used in [1] and [5],")
EQ(r"\rho_i\,\partial_t^2 u_i=\left(1+\tau_1\partial_t\right)\left[(\lambda_i+2\mu_i)\,\partial_x^2 u_i-\beta_i\,\partial_x\theta_i\right]", "mot2")
P("For $\\tau_1=0$ the classical coupled equation of motion is recovered. The same lag $\\tau_1$ is used in both layers, which is necessary for the stress to be continuous at the interface when the bracket is continuous. The lag is a model parameter, and its value is taken from the range of the other time scales of the problem and not from a measurement.")
H2("2.2 Fractional temperature-rate lag and the bioheat equation")
P("The fractional operator is the Caputo derivative. For a function $f$ and an order $\\gamma$ with $m-1<\\gamma\\le m$, where $m$ is an integer,")
EQ(r"D_t^{\gamma}f(t)=\frac{1}{\Gamma(m-\gamma)}\int_0^t (t-s)^{m-\gamma-1}\,\partial_s^{m}f(s)\,\mathrm{d}s", "caputo")
P("Its Laplace transform, with $\\hat f(s)$ the transform of $f$ and $s$ the transform variable, is")
EQ(r"\mathcal{L}\left[D_t^{\gamma}f\right]=s^{\gamma}\hat f(s)-\sum_{k=0}^{m-1}s^{\gamma-k-1}f^{(k)}(0)", "lapcap")
P("The quiescent initial state of the present problem makes all initial terms vanish for $\\gamma=1+\\alpha$, so that the fractional term transforms to $s^{1+\\alpha}\\hat f$ and the layered Laplace-domain solution remains exact. The fractional lag operator acting on a field $z$ is defined by")
EQ(r"\Lambda_\alpha z=\partial_t z+\tau_0^{\alpha}\,D_t^{1+\alpha}z,\qquad 0<\alpha\le1", "lam")
P("where $\\tau_0$ is the temperature-rate relaxation time and $\\tau_0^{\\alpha}$ carries the dimension that makes the second term homogeneous in time. For $\\alpha=1$ the Caputo derivative of order 2 is the second derivative, and the operator reduces to the Green–Lindsay form,")
EQ(r"\Lambda_1 z=\partial_t z+\tau_0\,\partial_t^2 z=\left(1+\tau_0\partial_t\right)\partial_t z", "lam1")
P("The heat flux obeys Fourier's law with a temperature-dependent conductivity $k_i(\\theta)$, and no lag is placed in the flux, which is the Green–Lindsay choice and which makes the flux continuous across the interface whenever $k\\partial_x\\theta$ is continuous:")
EQ(r"q_i=-k_i(\theta_i)\,\partial_x\theta_i", "flux")
P("The energy balance of the MGL theory then reads as follows. The lag operator acts on the heat content and on the thermoelastic coupling term, and it does not act on the external heat source or on the perfusion sink:")
EQ(r"-\partial_x q_i+Q-W_i(\theta_i)=\rho_ic_{vi}\Lambda_\alpha\theta_i+T_0\beta_i\Lambda_\alpha\left(\partial_x u_i\right)", "energy")
P("Two statements about this choice are needed. First, the fractional terms are the Caputo derivative of order $1+\\alpha$ of the temperature and of the dilatation, i.e. $\\tau_0^{\\alpha}D_t^{1+\\alpha}\\theta$ and $\\tau_0^{\\alpha}D_t^{1+\\alpha}e$ in Eq. (@energy@). The strain-rate lag $\\tau_1$, the perfusion sink and the source are integer-order. "
  "Second, leaving the perfusion sink outside the lag operator is a modelling decision. In the MGT form of the source model the lag operator multiplies the whole bracket, including the perfusion term, whereas in the Green–Lindsay form the lag multiplies the temperature rate only. The perfusion exchange between blood and tissue is a local process and is not part of the conduction mechanism, and this is why it is treated as an instantaneous sink here. "
  "For zero initial conditions the Caputo derivative of order $1+\\alpha$ equals the Caputo derivative of order $\\alpha$ of the first time derivative, which is the form implemented later:")
EQ(r"D_t^{1+\alpha}z=D_t^{\alpha}\left(\partial_t z\right)\quad\text{if}\quad z(0)=\partial_t z(0)=0", "equiv")
P("The memory interpretation of Eq. (@equiv@) is useful for the discussion later. The Caputo derivative of order $1+\\alpha$ of a field at rest equals a Riemann–Liouville integral of order $1-\\alpha$ of the second time derivative, so that the fractional lag term is a weighted average of the past accelerations of the temperature with a power-law kernel $(t-s)^{-\\alpha}$. A weight that decays slowly gives the tissue a long memory of its heating history, and the parameter $\\tau_0^{\\alpha}$ sets the strength of this memory. For $\\alpha=1$ the kernel collapses to a delta function and the memory disappears, leaving the instantaneous second derivative. This is why $\\alpha=1$ is the integer-order hyperbolic limit and why smaller $\\alpha$ moves the model towards diffusion in the sense of a heavier tail of the response, although the model does not reduce to the Pennes equation for any $\\alpha>0$ when $\\tau_0>0$.")
P("Combining Eqs. (@flux@) and (@energy@) gives the nonlinear fractional bioheat equation for each layer:")
EQ(r"\partial_x\left(k_i(\theta_i)\,\partial_x\theta_i\right)+Q-W_i(\theta_i)=\rho_ic_{vi}\Lambda_\alpha\theta_i+T_0\beta_i\Lambda_\alpha\left(\partial_x u_i\right)", "bio")
P("Setting $\\tau_0=0$, $\\tau_1=0$, $W_i=\\rho_bc_bw_{bi}\\theta_i$ and dropping the coupling gives the Pennes equation, and setting $\\alpha=1$ gives the integer-order MGL system. The limit $\\alpha\\to0$ is not used, because the operator then ceases to be a lag. For $0<\\alpha<1$ the large-$s$ behaviour of the Laplace-domain wave number is governed by $s^{(1+\\alpha)/2}$, which is slower than $s$, and the solution therefore has no sharp wavefront but a smooth precursor that is unbounded in speed. This is a property of the fractional operator that is verified numerically in Section 4.")
H2("2.3 Temperature-dependent conductivity and perfusion")
P("The conductivity is taken to depend linearly on the temperature increment, as in the source model, with the slope $\\chi_i$ and with $|\\chi_i\\theta_i|$ small. It is handled by the Kirchhoff transform, which turns the conduction term into a linear operator on a transformed variable:")
EQ(r"k_i(\theta)=k_{i0}\left(1+\chi_i\theta\right),\qquad \phi_i=\theta+\tfrac12\chi_i\theta^{2},\qquad k_i(\theta)\,\partial_x\theta=k_{i0}\,\partial_x\phi_i", "kirch")
P("The Kirchhoff variable guarantees that the flux $k_{i0}\\partial_x\\phi_i$ is the same quantity on both sides of the interface, and the finite element weak form uses it directly. The perfusion sink uses the blood properties $\\rho_b$ and $c_b$, the baseline perfusion rate $w_{bi0}$ and a dimensionless multiplier $\\omega_i(\\theta)$:")
EQ(r"W_i(\theta)=\omega_i(\theta)\,w_{bi0}\,\rho_bc_b\,\theta", "wb")
P("Two physical effects are represented by the multiplier. The perfusion rate rises with temperature because of vasodilation, and it falls to zero above a critical temperature rise when the vessels are damaged or compressed. A smooth law with both properties and with only three parameters per layer is")
EQ(r"\omega_i(\theta)=\frac{\left(1+c_{wi}\,\theta/\Delta T\right)S_i(\theta)}{S_i(0)},\qquad S_i(\theta)=\tfrac12\left[1-\tanh\frac{\theta/\Delta T-\theta_{ci}}{\Delta_c}\right]", "omega")
P("where $\\Delta T$ is the reference temperature rise, $c_{wi}$ is the vasodilation slope, $\\theta_{ci}$ is the dimensionless critical temperature rise and $\\Delta_c$ is the width of the shutdown transition. The division by $S_i(0)$ makes $\\omega_i(0)=1$ exactly. The law has the following limits:")
EQ(r"c_{wi}=0,\ \theta_{ci}\to\infty:\ \omega_i\equiv1\ (\text{Pennes});\qquad \theta\gg\theta_{ci}\Delta T:\ \omega_i\to0\ (\text{vascular stasis})", "omlim")
P("The parameters are illustrative. The values $c_{w1}=0.5$, $\\theta_{c1}=0.6$ for the tumour and $c_{w2}=3$, $\\theta_{c2}=0.9$ for the normal tissue encode the common observations that tumour vasculature responds weakly to heating and shuts down earlier, whereas normal tissue vasodilates strongly before it is damaged, and $\\Delta_c=0.1$ for both. "
  "With $\\Delta T=10$ K the critical temperatures correspond to the following values, which are model parameters and not clinical thresholds:")
EQ(r"T_{c1}=T_0+\theta_{c1}\Delta T=316\ \mathrm{K},\qquad T_{c2}=T_0+\theta_{c2}\Delta T=319\ \mathrm{K}", "tc")
P("Two properties of the law should be recorded. The multiplier is non-negative by construction, so that the perfusion term $W_i\\theta_i$ has the sign of $\\theta_i^2$ and removes energy from the tissue at every point where the temperature differs from the blood temperature. The law therefore cannot create energy, and the energy balance of the model remains dissipative with respect to the perfusion mechanism. Second, the product $\\omega(\\theta)\\theta$ is not monotone: it increases for small $\\theta$, reaches a maximum near the critical temperature and falls to zero in the stasis region. The sink is therefore a non-monotone function of temperature, and the steady-state problem of Section 3.5 may in principle have more than one solution for large perfusion numbers. In the computations below a single branch was found, and the Picard iteration always started from the solution of the previous time level, which follows the branch continuously.")
H2("2.4 Laser source")
P("The laser deposits heat with an exponential depth profile and a rectangular pulse of duration $\\tau_p$, in the form used in [13]:")
EQ(r"Q(x,t)=I_0\mu_a\,g(x)\,f(t),\qquad f(t)=H(t)-H(t-\tau_p)", "laser")
EQ(r"g(x)=B_1\,e^{-l_1x/\delta}-B_2\,e^{-l_2x/\delta},\qquad \delta=\frac{1}{\sqrt{3\mu_a\left(\mu_a+(1-g_a)\mu_s\right)}}", "gx")
P("where $I_0$ is the incident intensity, $\\mu_a$ and $\\mu_s$ are the absorption and scattering coefficients, $g_a$ is the anisotropy factor and $H$ is the Heaviside function. The coefficients depend on the diffuse reflectance $R_d$ through")
EQ(r"B_1=3.09+5.44R_d-2.12e^{-21.5R_d},\qquad B_2=2.09-1.47R_d-2.12e^{-21.5R_d}", "B")
EQ(r"l_1=1-\left(1-\tfrac{1}{\sqrt3}\right)e^{-20.1R_d},\qquad l_2=1.63\,e^{3.4R_d}", "ll")
P("For $R_d=0.05$ the values are $B_1=" + f3(LS["B1"]) + "$, $B_2=" + f3(LS["B2"]) + "$, $l_1=" + f3(LS["l1"]) + "$ and $l_2=" + f3(LS["l2"]) + "$, and the optical depth is $\\delta=" + g(LS["delta"] * 1e3, 4) + "$ mm.")
H2("2.5 Boundary, interface and initial conditions")
P("The tumour surface is traction free and is held at a raised temperature, and the far face is clamped and kept at the reference temperature:")
EQ(r"\sigma_1(0,t)=0,\qquad \theta_1(0,t)=\theta_0H(t)", "bc1")
EQ(r"u_2(L,t)=0,\qquad \theta_2(L,t)=0", "bc2")
P("At the interface the displacement, the stress, the temperature and the heat flux are continuous,")
EQ(r"u_1(h,t)=u_2(h,t),\qquad \sigma_1(h,t)=\sigma_2(h,t)", "if1")
EQ(r"\theta_1(h,t)=\theta_2(h,t),\qquad q_1(h,t)=q_2(h,t)", "if2")
P("and the medium is initially at rest in the reference state:")
EQ(r"u_i=\partial_tu_i=\theta_i=\partial_t\theta_i=0\quad\text{at}\quad t=0", "ic")
H2("2.6 Nondimensional form")
P("The nondimensionalisation of [13] is used. The length scale $l$ and the velocity scale $\\nu$ are")
EQ(r"\nu=\sqrt{\frac{\lambda_1+2\mu_1}{\rho_1}},\qquad l=\frac{k_{10}}{\rho_1c_{v1}\nu}", "scale")
P("The second relation is the choice that makes the coefficient $a_4$ in the tumour equal to one. The dimensionless variables, with $\\Delta T=T_\\infty-T_0$, are")
EQ(r"x^{*}=\frac{x}{l},\quad t^{*}=\frac{\nu t}{l},\quad u^{*}=\frac{(\lambda_1+2\mu_1)\,u}{l\,\beta_1\Delta T},\quad \theta^{*}=\frac{\theta}{\Delta T},\quad \sigma^{*}=\frac{\sigma}{\beta_1\Delta T}", "nd")
P("and the relaxation times are scaled in the same way as $t$; the asterisks are omitted from here on. The coefficients that appear are collected in the following relations. The density, stiffness, coupling, conductivity and heat capacity ratios are")
EQ(r"\hat\rho_i=\frac{\rho_i}{\rho_1},\quad a_{8i}=\frac{\lambda_i+2\mu_i}{\lambda_1+2\mu_1},\quad a_{9i}=\frac{\beta_i}{\beta_1},\quad \hat\kappa_i=\frac{k_{i0}}{k_{10}},\quad \hat C_i=\frac{\rho_ic_{vi}}{\rho_1c_{v1}}", "ratios")
P("The coefficients $a_1,\\dots,a_9$ of the source study that remain are")
EQ(r"a_{1i}=\frac{\lambda_i+2\mu_i}{\rho_i\nu^{2}},\qquad a_{2i}=\frac{\beta_i(\lambda_1+2\mu_1)}{\rho_i\nu^{2}\beta_1},\qquad a_{3i}=\frac{k^{*}_{i0}\,l}{k_{10}\,\nu}", "a123")
EQ(r"a_{4i}=\frac{\rho_ic_{vi}\,l\,\nu}{k_{10}},\qquad a_{5i}=\frac{T_0\beta_i\beta_1\,l\,\nu}{k_{10}(\lambda_1+2\mu_1)}", "a45")
EQ(r"a_{6i}=\frac{w_{bi0}\rho_bc_b\,l^{2}}{k_{10}},\qquad a_7=\frac{I_0\mu_a\,l^{2}}{k_{10}\Delta T}", "a67")
P("Their values are listed in Table 2. The coefficients $a_{1i}$ and $a_{2i}$ are equal to $a_{8i}/\\hat\\rho_i$ and $a_{9i}/\\hat\\rho_i$ and are given for reference to [13]. The model written in $\\hat\\rho_i$, $a_{8i}$, $a_{9i}$ keeps the divergence form, so that no derivative of a material property is needed in the weak form. The dimensionless motion equation and stress are")
EQ(r"\hat\rho_i\,\partial_t^2u_i=\partial_x\Sigma_i,\qquad \Sigma_i=\left(1+\tau_1\partial_t\right)S_i,\qquad S_i=a_{8i}\,\partial_xu_i-a_{9i}\,\theta_i", "ndmot")
P("where $S_i$ is the stress before the strain-rate operator acts and $\\Sigma_i=\\sigma_i^{*}$. The dimensionless energy equation, in Kirchhoff form, is")
EQ(r"\hat\kappa_i\,\partial_x^2\phi_i+a_7\,g\!\left(\frac{x}{\delta^{*}}\right)f(t)-a_{6i}\,\omega_i(\theta_i)\,\theta_i=\hat C_i\,\Lambda_\alpha\theta_i+a_{5i}\,\Lambda_\alpha\left(\partial_xu_i\right)", "ndenergy")
P("with $\\phi_i=\\theta_i+\\tfrac12\\chi_i\\theta_i^2$ and with $\\tau_0$ and $\\tau_p$ now dimensionless. The depth scale $\\delta^{*}=\\delta/l$ is introduced in the laser profile, and the boundary and interface conditions become")
EQ(r"\Sigma_1(0,t)=0,\ \ \theta_1(0,t)=\theta_0H(t),\ \ u_2(1,t)=0,\ \ \theta_2(1,t)=0", "ndbc")
EQ(r"[u]_h=0,\quad [\Sigma]_h=0,\quad [\theta]_h=0,\quad \left[\hat\kappa\,\partial_x\phi\right]_h=0", "ndif")
P("where $[\\cdot]_h$ denotes the jump across $x=h$. Two consequences of the scaling must be stated. First, with the numbers of Table 1 the length scale is $l=" + g(SC["l"] * 1e9, 3) + "$ nm and the time scale is $l/\\nu=" + g(SC["tl"] * 1e12, 3) + "$ ps, so that the dimensionless slab $L=1$ is only " + g(SC["l"] * 1e9, 3) + " nm thick and the optical depth is $\\delta^{*}=" + sc(LS["deltas"], 3) + "$. "
  "The laser profile is therefore uniform across the slab. Second, the dimensionless perfusion and laser numbers are $a_{61}=" + sc(a1["a6"], 3) + "$ and $a_7=" + sc(A7, 3) + "$, which means that the literal model has no measurable perfusion or laser effect. The relation between the perfusion number and a physical length is")
EQ(r"a_{61}=\left(\frac{l}{\ell_w}\right)^{2},\qquad \ell_w=\sqrt{\frac{k_{10}}{w_{b10}\rho_bc_b}}=" + g(math.sqrt(0.778 / (0.009 * 1060 * 3770)) * 1e3, 3) + r"\ \mathrm{mm}", "ellw")
P("so that a perfusion number of order one corresponds to a length scale of a few millimetres. In the studies of Section 4 the tumour perfusion number $\\Pi=a_{61}$ is therefore treated as a free parameter, and the normal-tissue value follows from the fixed ratio of the physical data,")
EQ(r"a_{62}=r_w\,\Pi,\qquad r_w=\frac{w_{b20}/k_{20}}{w_{b10}/k_{10}}=" + g(WR, 4), "rw")
P("This is a rescaling of one dimensionless group while the others are kept at their source values. It is an illustration of what the model does when perfusion is comparable to conduction, and it is not a statement about the time and length scales of a clinical treatment, for which both the relaxation times and the lengths would be many orders of magnitude different from the dimensionless values used here.")

FIG("Figure_1.png", "Schematic of the layered tumour and normal tissue slab, the finite element mesh with a node at the interface, and the solution procedure at each time step. This figure is a schematic and contains no computed data.")
TAB(*T1())
TAB(*T2())
